RG DS OS: run the SD card at the clock U-Boot asks for, not half of it.

Rockchip's dw_mmc (rk3288 and later, the RK3568 included) divides its input clock by 2 in its CLKGEN before the
core's CLKDIV, which is why Linux's dw_mmc-rockchip asks the CRU for twice the card clock (RK3288_CLKGEN_DIV).
U-Boot asks for the card clock itself, so with CLKDIV bypassed a 50 MHz high-speed card ran at 25 MHz: on the RG DS
a raw 10 MiB read took 0.872 s (12.0 MB/s, the 25 MHz limit), with the CRU at 50 MHz (CLKSEL_CON30 0x5420) and
CLKDIV 0 read back before boot (docs/OWN_BSP_PLAN.md, Phase E). A rate the CRU cannot make (800 kHz for the 400 kHz
identification clock) falls back to the old request, which the CRU rounds to 750 kHz as before.

--- a/drivers/mmc/rockchip_dw_mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/rockchip_dw_mmc.c
@@ -48,7 +48,11 @@
 	if (host->mmc->selected_mode == MMC_DDR_52 && host->mmc->bus_width == 8)
 		freq *= 2;

-	ret = clk_set_rate(&priv->clk, freq);
+	/* the controller's CLKGEN halves its input: ask the CRU for twice the clock (Linux's RK3288_CLKGEN_DIV) */
+	ret = clk_set_rate(&priv->clk, freq * 2);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		ret = clk_set_rate(&priv->clk, freq);
+
 	if (ret < 0) {
 		debug("%s: err=%d\n", __func__, ret);
 		return 0;
